SAT_CHUNK("rw/text-structure/2/1",[
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.73b26d357e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-0","passage":"<p>For decades, farmers in one river valley burned the stubble left in their rice fields after each harvest. Burning was fast and cheap, but it filled the air with smoke for weeks. When a local cooperative offered to buy the stubble instead, many farmers were skeptical that anyone would want it. Within three years, however, the cooperative was turning the straw into packaging material and selling it to companies across the region, and the autumn smoke had largely disappeared.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It compares two methods of growing rice and argues that one is more profitable.","It presents a debate among farmers and then explains why the debate remains unresolved.","It traces the history of rice farming in the valley from its beginnings to the present.","It describes a long-standing practice and its drawback, then explains how a new option changed that practice."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Old practice and its problem (smoke), then the cooperative's offer and its effects. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5648c8f304","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-3","passage":"<p>When the city opened its first public library branch in a converted bus station, officials expected it to be used mostly for borrowing books. Instead, the busiest part of the building turned out to be a row of tables with electrical outlets. Students came to charge laptops and do homework, job seekers used the free internet to fill out applications, and retirees gathered to read newspapers. Within a year, the library had added twice as many tables and cut back its shelf space.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that libraries should stop lending books.","It describes the history of a bus station and then explains why it was converted.","It lists the services libraries provide and ranks them by popularity.","It presents an expectation, describes what actually happened, and explains how the library responded."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Officials expected borrowing; people used the tables; the library adjusted. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.291af92fae","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-4","passage":"<p>Researchers studying a population of lizards on a small island noticed that the lizards' toe pads, which help them grip surfaces, were larger than those of the same species on the mainland. The island's vegetation is dominated by smooth-barked trees, while the mainland population lives mostly among rough rocks. The researchers suggest that larger toe pads may give the island lizards a better grip on the slick bark they must climb.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It questions whether the island lizards really have larger toe pads.","It introduces a second species of lizard for comparison.","It describes a difference in habitat that helps explain the difference in toe-pad size.","It explains how the researchers measured the lizards' toe pads."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Smooth bark versus rough rocks sets up the explanation in the final sentence. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4267778536","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-8","passage":"<p>Economists sometimes describe a \"cobra effect,\" in which a policy produces the opposite of its intended result. The term comes from a story, possibly exaggerated, about a reward once offered for dead cobras to reduce their numbers. Some residents reportedly began breeding cobras to collect the reward. When officials canceled the program, the breeders released their now-worthless snakes, leaving more cobras than before. Whether or not every detail is accurate, economists use the story to show how incentives can backfire.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"possibly exaggerated\" in the text?","choices":["It emphasizes how dangerous cobras are.","It indicates that economists invented the story.","It acknowledges that the story may not be completely accurate.","It suggests that the policy was more successful than reported."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase signals doubt about the story's details, echoed in \"whether or not every detail is accurate.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.57144911ea","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-11","passage":"<p>When a small publisher decided to print its novels on paper made from recycled cotton clothing, some booksellers worried that readers would dislike the paper's slightly rough texture. The publisher included a short note at the back of each book explaining where the paper came from. Sales of the new editions matched those of the old ones, and many readers wrote to say that the note had made them notice and appreciate the feel of the pages.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that all publishers should use recycled paper.","It explains how recycled paper is manufactured, step by step.","It describes a change, a concern about that change, and the outcome that followed.","It compares the prices of two kinds of paper."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The switch, booksellers' worry, then sales and reader responses. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.70cdcef9ed","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-13","passage":"<p>In the early years of the sport, marathon courses varied widely in length, from about 40 to 42 kilometers. Records set on one course could not be fairly compared with those set on another. In 1921, athletics officials adopted a standard distance of 42.195 kilometers, the length of a well-known course from a previous international competition. Only after that decision did marathon records become meaningful across different races.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a famous marathon runner's career from beginning to end.","It identifies a problem with early marathons and then describes the decision that solved it.","It compares marathon training methods across several decades.","It argues that marathons should be shorter."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Varied course lengths (problem), then the 1921 standard (solution). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d3844d099a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-15","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Grandfather's garden was a map of his life. The fig tree by the gate came from a cutting he had carried across an ocean wrapped in a damp shirt. The roses along the fence had been a gift on the day my mother was born. And the stubborn patch of mint, which he pulled up every spring and which grew back every summer, he said reminded him of himself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes a problem that the rest of the text resolves.","It introduces a character who appears only once in the text.","It states a central idea that the following details illustrate.","It contrasts the garden with another place in the novel."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The fig tree, roses, and mint each show how the garden maps his life. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.1d8a34e540","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-16","passage":"<p>Some city parks departments have begun replacing lawns with patches of native wildflowers and grasses. Lawns must be mowed, watered, and fertilized regularly, which is costly. Native plants, once established, need little water and almost no mowing. They also provide food for bees and butterflies. Critics argue that the plantings look untidy, but supporters point out that many residents have come to enjoy the changing colors through the seasons.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how native plants are established.","It presents an objection to the plantings along with a response to that objection.","It summarizes the costs of maintaining lawns.","It concludes that the plantings should be removed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Critics call them untidy; supporters note residents enjoy them. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.bd3897b187","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-17","passage":"<p>Early mapmakers often filled the unexplored edges of their maps with drawings of sea monsters. Historians once treated these images as simple decoration or evidence of superstition. One historian has proposed a different reading: many of the creatures closely resemble descriptions from sailors' reports of whales, walruses, and giant squid. On this view, the drawings were mapmakers' attempts, however imperfect, to record real animals that travelers had described.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a feature of early maps, gives a traditional interpretation of it, and then presents an alternative interpretation.","It argues that modern maps are less accurate than early maps.","It explains how early maps were made and then lists their errors.","It describes a sailor's voyage and then analyzes the map he used."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Feature (sea monsters), old view (decoration/superstition), new view (records of real animals). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3c6c4673df","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data1-20","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father never told me he was proud of me. He was not a man who said such things. But every newspaper clipping that mentioned my name, even a single line about a spelling bee, ended up in a folder in his desk drawer. I found the folder after he died. It was two inches thick.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that the folder contained mostly unrelated articles.","It reveals that the narrator was disappointed by what she found.","It emphasizes how carefully and extensively the father had followed the narrator's accomplishments.","It explains why the father never spoke about his feelings."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The folder's thickness underscores how much he collected. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.98b5c08dad","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data2-29","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, the carnival arrived in town on the same Tuesday and left on the same Sunday. Nadia had watched it come and go for eleven years without ever riding the Ferris wheel. This year, standing in line with a ticket that had cost her two weeks of saved allowance, she felt her heart pounding. When the attendant waved her forward, she stepped into the swaying seat before she could change her mind.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the Ferris wheel's size and design.","It establishes a long history of avoiding the ride, which makes her decision this year significant.","It explains how the carnival's schedule has changed over time.","It suggests that Nadia has never been to the carnival before."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Eleven years without riding gives weight to this year's choice. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.12ae68ed0d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data2-30","passage":"<p>For centuries, people in some mountain villages have built terraces, flat steps carved into steep hillsides, to grow crops. Terraces slow the flow of rainwater so that it soaks into the soil instead of rushing downhill and carrying the soil away. In several regions where terraces were abandoned as families moved to cities, landslides and erosion increased within a few decades.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It contradicts the claim made in the second sentence.","It provides evidence of the terraces' protective role by describing what happened without them.","It describes how terraces are built.","It explains why families moved from the villages to cities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Increased erosion after abandonment supports the terraces' protective function. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.33635b45b3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data2-31","passage":"<p>A composer who writes music for video games explained that her work differs from writing for film in one important way: in a film, every scene lasts exactly as long as the director decides, but in a game, the player controls how long each moment lasts. A player might spend thirty seconds or thirty minutes exploring a single room. As a result, she writes pieces that can loop smoothly or shift gradually to match what the player is doing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the composer's childhood and then her career.","It argues that film music is superior to game music.","It compares the budgets of films and video games.","It identifies a difference between two kinds of composing and explains how that difference shapes the composer's work."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Film timing versus player-controlled timing, then the resulting approach. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.8f84d245fb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data2-34","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from a nineteenth-century letter.</p><p>My dear sister, the voyage has been long, and the sea has not been kind. For nine days the waves rose higher than the mast, and I confess I did not expect to write to you again. Yet this morning the sky cleared, a flock of gulls circled the ship, and the captain says we shall see land within two days. I have never been so glad to hear the cry of a bird.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a period of danger and then a moment of hope.","It describes the writer's plans after arriving on land.","It lists the ship's supplies and then describes the crew.","It compares two voyages the writer has taken."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Nine days of storms, then \"yet this morning the sky cleared.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3ca1ace1ed","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data2-37","passage":"<p>A marine biologist studying cleaner wrasses, small fish that eat parasites off larger fish, noticed that the wrasses behaved differently when other fish were watching. When observed by potential clients, the wrasses were more careful to eat only parasites rather than nipping at the healthy mucus on their clients' skin, which they prefer. The biologist suggests that the wrasses may be managing their reputations.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It offers an interpretation of the behavior described earlier in the text.","It describes the method the biologist used.","It explains what cleaner wrasses eat.","It presents a finding that contradicts the earlier observations."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The reputation idea explains why behavior changes when others watch. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4814594388","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-39","passage":"<p>When the town of Millbrook lost its only grocery store, residents who did not own cars faced a forty-minute bus ride to buy fresh food. A group of retirees responded by organizing a weekly market in the church parking lot, inviting farmers from nearby counties to sell produce directly. The market began with four vendors. Five years later it hosts thirty, and the town council has voted to make the parking lot a permanent public market space.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It presents a problem, describes a community response, and then shows how that response developed over time.","It argues that towns should not rely on grocery stores.","It compares two grocery stores and explains why one closed.","It describes the history of a church and its parking lot."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Problem (no store), response (market), development (thirty vendors, permanent space). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3020318924","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-40","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every evening at six, Mr. Hadley walked the length of Harbor Road with his hands clasped behind his back, nodding to each neighbor by name. When he fell ill one winter and the walks stopped, no one mentioned it at first. But by the third evening, doors along Harbor Road began to open, and one neighbor after another found a reason to carry soup, or a newspaper, or simply a question, to the small blue house at the end of the street.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why Mr. Hadley became ill.","It establishes a routine whose interruption drives the rest of the text.","It introduces a conflict between Mr. Hadley and his neighbors.","It describes the layout of Harbor Road in detail."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The daily walk sets up the change when \"the walks stopped.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.345e103b38","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-43","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kettle hums before it sings, / a low and patient sound, / as if it knows the morning / needs a little time to come around. / And I, who rush from bed to door, / could stand to learn its art: / to warm up slowly, steadily, / before I make a start.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes an object's behavior and then applies a lesson from that behavior to the speaker's own life.","It narrates a sequence of events from a busy morning.","It describes a sound and then explains its scientific cause.","It compares two kettles and chooses the better one."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The first half describes the kettle; the second half turns to the speaker. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5428c48354","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-44","passage":"<p>In many cities, rooftop gardens are praised for cooling buildings and reducing stormwater runoff. A research team measuring rooftops in one city confirmed both benefits but found a third that residents had not expected: the gardens attracted dozens of species of bees, including several rarely seen elsewhere in the city. The researchers now argue that rooftop gardens should be designed with pollinators in mind, using flowering plants that bloom at different times of year.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how the researchers counted the bees.","It restates the benefits described in the first sentence.","It presents a recommendation that follows from the unexpected finding.","It questions whether the study's measurements were accurate."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The recommendation builds on the discovery of many bee species. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.73b7974d5a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-46","passage":"<p>Hummingbirds have one of the highest metabolic rates of any animal and must eat almost constantly while awake. At night, however, many species enter a state called torpor, lowering their body temperature and heart rate dramatically. A hummingbird in torpor can use a small fraction of the energy it would need to stay fully active overnight, allowing it to survive until it can feed again in the morning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It compares hummingbirds with other birds.","It establishes the energy demands that make torpor useful.","It describes what happens to hummingbirds during torpor.","It argues that hummingbirds should be fed at night."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Their constant need for food explains why saving energy at night matters. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.501515df6f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-47","passage":"<p>In the early twentieth century, a city built its main library with enormous windows on every side, at a time when most libraries relied on small windows and gas lamps. Critics complained that the glass would make the building cold and fade the books. The architect responded by placing reading tables near the windows and shelving the books in the building's interior, away from direct sunlight. The library's reading room became one of the most popular public spaces in the city.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It provides evidence that the critics were correct.","It describes the library's popularity.","It describes how the architect addressed one of the critics' concerns.","It explains why gas lamps were common in libraries."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shelving books away from sunlight answers the worry about fading. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0eb40a0558","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-48","passage":"<p>A psychologist asked participants to predict how happy they would feel a year after a major event, such as moving to a new city. Most predicted that their happiness would change a great deal, for better or worse. When the psychologist checked in a year later, most participants' happiness had returned close to their original levels. The study adds to evidence that people tend to overestimate how long the emotional effects of events will last.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a debate between two psychologists.","It describes participants' predictions, compares them with what later happened, and states a broader conclusion.","It lists several major life events in order of importance.","It explains how to become happier after moving."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Predictions, follow-up results, then the general conclusion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.c17a0f47d4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-50","passage":"<p>In one study, researchers placed identical bowls of candy on desks in an office, some in clear bowls and some in opaque bowls. Over several weeks, employees ate noticeably more candy from the clear bowls. When the researchers moved the clear bowls just two meters away from the desks, candy consumption dropped sharply. The findings suggest that small changes in how visible and convenient food is can significantly affect how much people eat.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the researchers chose clear bowls.","It presents a second finding that shows the effect of convenience.","It describes the type of candy used.","It contradicts the finding presented in the second sentence."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Distance, like visibility, changed consumption. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4c295389f2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-51","passage":"<p>For centuries, residents of a desert region have built underground channels that carry water from distant mountain aquifers to their villages. Because the channels run beneath the surface, little water is lost to evaporation, even in extreme heat. Maintaining the channels requires workers to climb down vertical shafts to clear sand and repair walls, a skill traditionally passed from parents to children. As fewer young people learn the trade, some channels have fallen into disrepair.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It summarizes the benefits of the channels.","It explains how the channels were first built.","It introduces a threat to the system described earlier in the text.","It describes where the aquifers are located."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Fewer workers and disrepair present a problem. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.baad946921","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-54","passage":"<p>A city once required new buildings to include large parking garages, reasoning that plenty of parking would reduce traffic on crowded streets. Urban researchers later studied the policy's effects. They found that abundant, cheap parking encouraged more people to drive downtown, adding to congestion, while the garages took up space that could have been used for housing or shops. After reviewing the research, the city eliminated the requirement in its central district.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It compares traffic in two cities over several decades.","It argues that no one should drive downtown.","It explains how parking garages are designed.","It describes a policy and its reasoning, presents research on its effects, and reports the resulting change."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Policy, research findings, and policy change. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ba3ecdfb90","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-56","passage":"<p>Many historians once believed that a certain medieval trade route declined because of a single event: the collapse of a major bridge. More recent work draws on tax records, merchant letters, and archaeological surveys of roadside inns. Together they suggest a gradual decline that began decades before the bridge fell, as new sea routes offered cheaper and faster ways to move goods. The bridge's collapse, in this view, hastened a change already under way.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that archaeological surveys are unreliable.","It presents an earlier explanation, describes new evidence, and offers a revised explanation.","It lists goods carried along the trade route.","It describes how medieval bridges were built."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Old view (bridge), new sources, revised view (gradual decline). The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7041d13b7c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-57","passage":"<p>Crows are known for their intelligence, but a study of city crows revealed a particularly surprising ability. Researchers wearing a distinctive mask trapped and released a few crows. For years afterward, crows in the area scolded and dive-bombed anyone wearing that mask, including crows that had never been trapped, while ignoring researchers in other masks. The birds had apparently learned to recognize a dangerous face and passed that knowledge to others.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"including crows that had never been trapped\" in the text?","choices":["It indicates that the reaction to the mask spread beyond the crows that had direct experience with it.","It suggests that the researchers trapped every crow in the area.","It explains how the researchers chose the mask.","It shows that the crows reacted to all masks equally."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Untrapped crows reacting supports the idea that knowledge was passed on. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d339747759","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-58","passage":"<p>In the 1950s, engineers designing a new highway through a valley proposed cutting down an old grove of oak trees. Local residents objected, noting that the grove was among the last remnants of the forest that once covered the valley. After months of debate, the engineers shifted the highway's route slightly to the east. The adjustment added a few hundred meters to the road, but today the grove is a protected park crossed by walking trails.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the original route of the highway.","It argues that highways should never be rerouted.","It explains why residents objected to the plan.","It describes a cost of the decision and its lasting benefit."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A slightly longer road, but a protected park today. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2f1ef2cb9e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-59","passage":"<p>Children learning to read often sound out unfamiliar words letter by letter. Skilled adult readers, by contrast, recognize most familiar words instantly as whole units. Researchers studying eye movements have found, however, that even skilled readers slow down and fix their gaze longer on rare or unfamiliar words, suggesting that the older strategy of decoding piece by piece never disappears entirely but is used when needed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It lists several methods for teaching reading.","It contrasts two reading strategies and then presents a finding that complicates the contrast.","It explains why children dislike reading aloud.","It describes a reading lesson and evaluates its success."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Children decode, adults recognize words, \"however\" skilled readers still decode when needed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7841e335e9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-60","passage":"<p>When a regional orchestra noticed that few young people attended its concerts, it began offering a free \"pay what you wish\" performance each month. Critics warned that people would come once for free and never buy full-price tickets. Two years of sales data showed a different pattern: many first-time attendees at the free concerts later returned to regular concerts, and the average age of subscribers dropped by nearly a decade.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why young people rarely attended concerts.","It presents a prediction that the data described in the final sentence contradict.","It provides evidence that supports the orchestra's plan.","It describes how the orchestra set its ticket prices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Critics predicted no return visits; data showed many returned. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.51d6f5a372","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-61","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My mother kept a garden that never looked like the ones in magazines. Tomato cages leaned at angles, the beans climbed a fence made from old bed frames, and the marigolds grew wherever their seeds happened to fall. Visitors sometimes offered to help her \"fix it up.\" She would smile, hand them a basket, and send them out to pick whatever was ripe.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the mother's plan to rebuild the garden.","It presents a visitor's criticism of the garden.","It explains why the mother started gardening.","It provides details that illustrate how the garden differed from magazine gardens."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Leaning cages, bed-frame fence, and scattered marigolds show its untidiness. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.9a8fe84f97","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-62","passage":"<p>Many coastal towns once dumped the shells left over from seafood processing into landfills. In one bay, a restoration group began collecting oyster shells from restaurants and returning them to the water in mesh bags. Young oysters need hard surfaces to attach to, and the recycled shells gave them a place to settle. Within a few years, new reefs had formed, filtering the water and providing habitat for fish and crabs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a problem with the restoration project.","It describes how restaurants prepare oysters.","It compares oysters with other shellfish.","It explains why returning shells to the water helps oysters."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Young oysters need hard surfaces, which the shells provide. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0e708131bf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-64","passage":"<p>A school district tested a program in which high school students tutored younger children in reading twice a week. As expected, the younger children's reading scores improved. Less expected was the effect on the tutors: their own reading comprehension scores rose more than those of classmates who did not participate, and many reported feeling more confident speaking in class.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes a weakness of the program.","It restates the expected result described in the second sentence.","It explains how tutors were selected.","It presents an unexpected finding about the program's effects."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Less expected was the effect on the tutors.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.36345a3f1a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-65","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter had sat unopened on Tomas's desk for three days. He knew the university's decision was inside. Each morning he picked it up, weighed it in his hand as if its thickness could tell him the answer, and set it down again. On the fourth morning his little sister marched in, tore it open before he could stop her, and read the first line aloud with a shriek of delight.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It breaks the tension built up in the earlier sentences.","It reveals that the letter was sent to the wrong person.","It describes Tomas's desk in detail.","It explains why Tomas applied to the university."],"answer":"A","explanation":"After days of hesitation, the sister opens the letter. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0116b84b06","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data3-67","passage":"<p>Some languages have many more words for colors than others. For years, researchers debated whether speakers of such languages actually perceive colors differently. In one experiment, speakers of a language with separate words for light blue and dark blue were slightly faster at telling those two shades apart than speakers of a language with only one word for blue. The difference was small, but it suggests that the words people use may subtly influence how quickly they notice distinctions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"The difference was small\" in the text?","choices":["It indicates that the experiment failed.","It qualifies the finding so that its conclusion is not overstated.","It contradicts the claim in the first sentence.","It explains how the shades of blue were chosen."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It limits the strength of the claim, which uses \"may subtly influence.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.bc915fb514","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-70","passage":"<p>Researchers wondered whether dogs understand where a person is pointing or simply follow the movement of a hand. In one test, a researcher pointed at one of two cups while standing still, then lowered her arm before the dog was released. Most dogs still went to the indicated cup, even though the arm was no longer moving or extended. The results suggest that dogs interpret pointing as a signal about a location, not merely as a motion to follow.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It compares several breeds of dog.","It argues that dogs are smarter than all other animals.","It describes how to train a dog to find hidden objects.","It poses a question, describes a test designed to answer it, and states what the results suggest."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Question, experimental design, result, and conclusion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.cb399a97c2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-71","passage":"<p>When the price of a city's public transit passes was cut in half for residents over sixty-five, officials expected ridership among older residents to rise modestly. It nearly doubled. Surveys revealed that many older residents had wanted to use transit more but had been deterred by cost. Several also reported visiting friends, libraries, and parks more often, suggesting that the policy affected not only transportation but also social life.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the sentence \"It nearly doubled\" in the text?","choices":["It describes how officials planned the policy.","It indicates that the policy failed.","It explains why transit passes were expensive.","It shows that the actual result far exceeded officials' expectations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Officials expected a modest rise. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.030724f17b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-73","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay about walking.</p><p>I used to measure a walk by its destination: the store, the office, the train. Then an injury took away my car for a season, and I began walking everywhere, slowly. I learned which porch held a sleeping cat, which corner smelled of bread at seven, which tree dropped its leaves first. The destinations were the same, but the walks had become places in themselves.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the writer's earlier view, an event that changed it, and the new perspective that resulted.","It compares walking with cycling.","It gives directions to several locations in a town.","It explains how the writer recovered from an injury."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Old view (destination), injury, and new appreciation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5dfe6b9113","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-74","passage":"<p>Many people assume that the Great Pyramid's builders used slaves forced to work under harsh conditions. Excavations near the pyramid, however, have uncovered workers' villages with bakeries, breweries, and cemeteries where laborers were buried with bread and beer for the afterlife. Some skeletons show healed injuries that suggest the workers received medical care. These findings have led many archaeologists to conclude that the builders were paid laborers who were respected for their work.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second and third sentences in the text as a whole?","choices":["They describe how the pyramid was built.","They explain why the pyramid was built.","They support the assumption described in the first sentence.","They present evidence that challenges the assumption described in the first sentence."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Bakeries, respectful burials, and medical care contradict the slave assumption. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.950eb051b8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-78","passage":"<p>When a large bookstore chain opened near a small independent bookshop, many predicted that the smaller store would close within a year. Instead, the owner began hosting weekly author readings, a children's story hour, and a book club that met in the shop after hours. Sales of new books dipped slightly, but sales of books connected to events rose, and the shop celebrated its twentieth anniversary a decade later.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the chain store chose that location.","It summarizes the shop's anniversary celebration.","It presents a prediction that the rest of the text shows to be wrong.","It describes the history of the independent shop."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Many predicted closure; instead the shop thrived. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.496fb6ffbe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-81","passage":"<p>Roads built through forests can divide wildlife habitats, making it dangerous for animals to cross. In one mountain region, engineers built bridges covered with soil and native plants over a busy highway. Motion cameras showed deer, bears, and even small mammals using the crossings regularly. Collisions between vehicles and large animals on that stretch of highway dropped by more than eighty percent.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It explains how motion cameras work.","It compares highways in two countries.","It describes a problem, a solution that was tried, and evidence of the solution's success.","It describes the habitats of several animals."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Divided habitats, bridges, and camera and collision data. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f088ea5b5a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-82","passage":"<p>A researcher analyzing thousands of restaurant reviews noticed that reviewers used different words depending on price. In reviews of expensive restaurants, words associated with long, complex descriptions appeared more often, while reviews of inexpensive places more often compared food to comforting or addictive things. The researcher suggests that people may describe food partly to signal something about themselves, not only to describe the meal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how the reviews were collected.","It gives specific examples of the pattern mentioned in the first sentence.","It presents a finding that contradicts the first sentence.","It describes the researcher's favorite restaurant."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It details how word choice differed by price. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.8e3f10319a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-83","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandfather's hands were maps of work, / the knuckles hills, the scars like rivers crossed. / He never told me where he'd been, / but I could read it there: / the rope burns of the fishing years, / the calluses from building walls, / the pale half-moon a saw once carved / the winter the barn was raised.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how the poem develops its central comparison?","choices":["It describes a barn and then explains how it was built.","It compares the grandfather to a river and describes a journey.","It compares two people's hands and judges which worked harder.","It compares the grandfather's hands to maps and then identifies specific marks that record his experiences."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Maps of work,\" followed by rope burns, calluses, and a scar. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.755d905780","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-84","passage":"<p>Many schools ban phones in classrooms, arguing that the devices distract students. One district tested a policy requiring students to store phones in lockers during the school day. Over a year, teachers reported fewer interruptions and students' test scores rose slightly, with the largest gains among students who had previously struggled. Some students, however, reported feeling anxious without access to their phones, especially in the first weeks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the district adopted the policy.","It presents a drawback that complicates the policy's otherwise positive results.","It describes how phones were stored.","It summarizes the test score gains."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"However\" introduces student anxiety. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.694f27cdc2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-87","passage":"<p>For decades, a popular brand of breakfast cereal was sold in a tall box. When the company switched to a shorter, wider box containing the same amount of cereal, shoppers complained that they were getting less. Sales dipped until the company printed the weight in large numbers on the front and added a line stating \"Same amount, new shape.\" Sales then recovered.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a change, a problem it caused, and how the problem was resolved.","It argues that tall boxes are always better.","It compares two cereal brands.","It explains how cereal is manufactured."],"answer":"A","explanation":"New box, complaints and falling sales, and labeling that fixed it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7d5853dfa8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-88","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The storm had passed, but the town was silent. No cars, no radios, no hum of refrigerators. Luis walked outside and heard things he had never noticed: water dripping from the gutter in a steady rhythm, a dog barking three streets away, his neighbor laughing on her porch. He stood there a long time, listening to a town he thought he knew.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It introduces a conflict between Luis and his neighbor.","It explains the absence of everyday noise that makes the new sounds noticeable.","It lists the objects Luis owns.","It describes damage caused by the storm."],"answer":"B","explanation":"No cars, radios, or refrigerators. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b9e3adcf07","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-89","passage":"<p>Economists studying a coastal fishing town found that when the government set strict limits on how many fish each boat could catch, conflicts among fishers initially increased. Over time, however, fish populations recovered, catches became more predictable, and fishers' average incomes rose above their levels before the limits. Many fishers who had opposed the rules came to support them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the word \"however\" in the text?","choices":["It contrasts the fishing town with other towns.","It signals a shift from the limits' early difficulties to their later benefits.","It introduces a reason the limits were created.","It indicates that the fish populations never recovered."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Initial conflict, \"however,\" recovery and higher incomes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5d3b0dcdc9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data4-94","passage":"<p>Many museums keep only a small fraction of their collections on display, storing the rest in warehouses. One natural history museum decided to let visitors view part of its storage area through glass walls, where scientists could be seen cataloging specimens. Visitor surveys showed that many people found the working storage area more interesting than some traditional exhibits, because it showed how museum research is actually done.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how specimens are cataloged.","It argues that museums should stop collecting specimens.","It provides background that explains why opening storage areas could show visitors something new.","It describes the results of the visitor surveys."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Most collections are hidden in storage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ae13f0bb12","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-97","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Aunt Rosa's kitchen radio had been broken for years, stuck forever on a station that played old dance music. Everyone offered to buy her a new one. She refused. When I asked her why, she turned up the volume, took my hands, and spun me across the linoleum floor, laughing, until the song ended. \"Now,\" she said, slightly out of breath, \"you tell me what's broken.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reveals that Aunt Rosa has finally agreed to replace the radio.","It explains how the radio became stuck on one station.","It suggests that Aunt Rosa sees the radio's limitation as unimportant compared with the pleasure it gives.","It introduces a disagreement between Aunt Rosa and the narrator."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Her question invites the narrator to rethink what \"broken\" means. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.c6566557bd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-99","passage":"<p>In the 1800s, many doctors did not wash their hands between examining patients. One physician noticed that women giving birth in a hospital ward staffed by doctors died of fever far more often than women in a ward staffed by midwives, who did not perform autopsies. He suspected that doctors were carrying something harmful from autopsies to patients and required them to wash their hands with a chlorine solution. Death rates in the doctors' ward fell dramatically.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the physician's early life.","It shows that the physician's idea was wrong.","It describes a comparison that led the physician to suspect a cause.","It explains how chlorine solutions were made."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The difference between wards pointed to autopsies. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4a50618736","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-101","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>I learned to swim in a cold lake at the age of nine, taught by my older brother, who was eleven and far more confident than he had any right to be. His method was simple: he stood waist-deep and told me to swim to him. Each time I got close, he took one step back. I was furious. By the end of the summer, I could swim across the entire cove.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the sentence \"I was furious\" in the text?","choices":["It shows that the brother was angry with the narrator.","It explains why the narrator stopped swimming.","It conveys the narrator's childhood reaction to a method that later proved effective.","It describes the temperature of the lake."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Her anger contrasts with her eventual success. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ea01efeb21","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-102","passage":"<p>Many people believe that bulls are enraged by the color red. In fact, cattle cannot distinguish red from green. Experiments have shown that bulls charge at a moving cape regardless of its color, suggesting that the motion, not the color, provokes them. The red cape's use in bullfighting likely has more to do with tradition and showmanship than with any effect on the animal.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the history of bullfighting in detail.","It argues that bulls should be trained differently.","It presents a common belief, provides evidence against it, and offers another explanation for a related practice.","It compares the eyesight of several animals."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Belief about red, evidence of color blindness and motion, and tradition as the explanation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4a4e387774","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-104","passage":"<p>Scientists once assumed that plants grow toward light simply because light speeds up growth. Later experiments revealed a more precise mechanism. A plant hormone gathers on the shaded side of a stem, causing cells there to lengthen faster than cells on the sunny side. Because the shaded side grows more, the stem bends toward the light.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It presents two theories and declares both incorrect.","It describes how to grow plants indoors.","It describes an earlier assumption and then explains the mechanism that later experiments revealed.","It compares plants with animals."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Assumption, then hormone mechanism. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7fb85f4df8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-105","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The chess club had lost every match that season. At the final tournament, twelve-year-old Imani faced the league's top player, a high school senior who had not lost a game in two years. Forty minutes in, the senior frowned, studied the board for a long time, and then extended his hand across the table. The room went silent. Imani stared at his hand before she understood.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how Imani learned to play chess.","It establishes the club's poor record, making Imani's result more surprising.","It describes the rules of the tournament.","It reveals the outcome of the final match."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A losing season heightens the surprise of the win. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f47d019b58","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-106","passage":"<p>When a river city experienced repeated floods, planners considered building taller levees. Instead, they converted a strip of low-lying land along the river into a park designed to flood safely, with grassy fields and paths that could be submerged without serious damage. During heavy rains, the park absorbs overflow that would otherwise reach nearby streets. In dry months, residents use it for sports and picnics.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It questions whether the park was effective.","It describes damage caused by recent floods.","It explains why the city chose not to build levees.","It describes an additional benefit of the park beyond flood control."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sports and picnics add value in dry months. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3cea7d7733","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-109","passage":"<p>A study of college students found that those who wrote down three things they were grateful for each week reported higher life satisfaction after ten weeks than students who wrote about daily hassles or neutral events. The effect was modest, and the researchers cautioned that the study relied on students' own reports of how they felt.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why gratitude improves health.","It describes how students were chosen for the study.","It presents a second finding that contradicts the first.","It qualifies the study's finding by noting its limitations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It notes a modest effect and reliance on self-reports. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.c0c67e5123","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-111","passage":"<p>In the early years of aviation, many engineers tried to build flying machines that flapped their wings like birds. These designs rarely worked. Later inventors achieved sustained flight by separating two functions that birds' wings combine: fixed wings provided lift, while propellers provided forward thrust. Studying birds was useful, but copying them too literally had led engineers astray.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that engineers should stop studying birds.","It explains how propellers work.","It states a general lesson drawn from the history described earlier.","It describes the first successful flight."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Studying birds helped, but literal copying misled engineers. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.a29591e9f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-112","passage":"<p>Researchers tracking city foxes with GPS collars found that the animals adjusted their routines to avoid people. Foxes that lived near busy parks were active mainly after midnight, while foxes in quieter suburbs often moved about in the early evening. The researchers noted that the foxes' diets also differed, with park foxes eating more discarded human food.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how the GPS collars worked.","It contradicts the finding about activity times.","It describes why foxes live in cities.","It adds another difference between the two groups of foxes."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Diet is a second difference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d4d711fda2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-113","passage":"<p>A newspaper columnist argued that handwriting instruction wastes class time now that most writing is done on keyboards. A literacy researcher responded with evidence from studies of young children. Children who practiced forming letters by hand recognized letters more quickly and remembered spellings better than children who practiced only by typing. Handwriting, the researcher concluded, may support reading and spelling even if adults later write mostly by keyboard.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It explains how newspapers choose columnists.","It describes the history of keyboards.","It presents an argument, introduces a response, and describes the evidence and conclusion supporting that response.","It compares two methods of teaching adults."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Columnist's claim, researcher's evidence and conclusion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.1e9558cf41","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-114","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The oak does not hurry. / It adds a ring a year, / a thin brown line of patience / no one sees. / Storms come, and summers, / and the children who climbed it / grow tall and move away, / and still it adds its ring.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final line of the poem?","choices":["It reveals that the tree has died.","It emphasizes the tree's persistence by repeating the image of adding a ring.","It introduces a new character.","It describes the children's return."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"And still it adds its ring\" echoes the second line. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b96b2a6170","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-115","passage":"<p>Many people think of deserts as places with no water, but some desert animals obtain nearly all the moisture they need from food. Kangaroo rats, for example, rarely drink. They get water from seeds, and their kidneys produce extremely concentrated urine, conserving what little water they take in. They also spend the hottest hours in cool burrows, reducing moisture lost through breathing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second, third, and fourth sentences in the text as a whole?","choices":["They provide an example that supports the claim in the first sentence.","They explain how deserts form.","They compare kangaroo rats with camels.","They present evidence against the claim in the first sentence."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Kangaroo rats illustrate animals that get water from food. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.03d95ee965","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-117","passage":"<p>Paleontologists once imagined large dinosaurs as slow, cold-blooded animals that dragged their tails. Evidence from fossil trackways, which show tails held off the ground, and from bone growth rings, which suggest rapid growth, has changed that view. Many scientists now picture dinosaurs as active animals, some possibly warm-blooded, with tails held out for balance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes how fossils form.","It compares dinosaurs with modern reptiles.","It explains why dinosaurs became extinct.","It describes an older view, presents evidence that changed it, and states the newer view."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Old view, evidence, and new view. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5cc041a36f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-121","passage":"<p>Some schools have replaced traditional letter grades with detailed written feedback. Supporters argue that grades encourage students to focus on scores rather than learning. In one study, students who received only comments on their essays improved more on later assignments than students who received grades alone. Students who received both grades and comments, however, improved about as little as those who received grades alone, apparently paying attention mainly to the grade.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a finding that suggests grades can reduce the benefit of comments.","It explains why schools use letter grades.","It describes how the essays were scored.","It restates the finding in the third sentence."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Grades plus comments performed like grades alone. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.72e69d5d22","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-122","passage":"<p>Coastal fog is an important water source for some redwood forests. Scientists measuring moisture found that during dry summers, redwoods absorb water from fog through their leaves, and fog dripping from branches can supply a substantial share of the water reaching the forest floor. Researchers are concerned that warming ocean temperatures could reduce fog along the coast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It contradicts the scientists' findings.","It explains how scientists measured moisture.","It raises a concern that follows from the importance of fog described earlier.","It describes redwoods' height."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Less fog would matter because redwoods depend on it. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.a973470da3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data5-123","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The science fair judges had moved on, and Dario's volcano had not erupted. He had checked the vinegar and the baking soda twice. His friends drifted away to watch a robot that played music. Alone at his table, Dario lifted the lid of the volcano and saw it: a folded note from his little sister, taped inside, blocking the tube. It read, \"Good luck!!!\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It explains why the judges chose the winners.","It compares Dario's project with his friends' projects.","It describes a disappointing failure and then reveals its unexpected cause.","It describes how to build a volcano model step by step."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The failure, then the note's discovery. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.387b959b8b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-124","passage":"<p>When a hospital added a small garden where patients could sit outdoors, staff expected it to be used mainly by visitors. Instead, nurses began bringing patients recovering from surgery outside for short periods. Records later showed that these patients requested less pain medication on average than similar patients who stayed indoors, though the researchers noted that the patients who went outside may have been healthier to begin with.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final clause, beginning with \"though,\" in the text?","choices":["It provides additional evidence that the garden reduced pain.","It explains why the garden was built.","It acknowledges a possible alternative explanation for the finding.","It describes how nurses chose which patients to bring outside."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Healthier patients might have needed less medication anyway. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.99ec59157f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-126","passage":"<p>Scientists studying bamboo noticed that many species flower only once every several decades, and when they do, nearly all plants of the species flower at the same time, even in different countries. After flowering, the plants die. The simultaneous flowering produces an enormous quantity of seeds. Researchers suggest that this strategy may overwhelm seed-eating animals, which cannot eat all the seeds, allowing many to sprout.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes an unusual pattern and then presents a possible explanation for it.","It compares bamboo with trees.","It explains how to grow bamboo at home.","It argues that bamboo should be protected."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Pattern, then the explanation about overwhelming seed eaters. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2770ce517c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-127","passage":"<p>In the 1970s, a city tore down an elevated highway that divided its waterfront from downtown. Many business owners predicted traffic chaos. Traffic did shift to other streets, but congestion did not increase as much as feared, partly because some drivers changed their routes or schedules and some switched to transit. The land along the water became a popular promenade lined with restaurants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a prediction that later developments did not fully support.","It describes the promenade's restaurants.","It explains why the highway was built.","It provides evidence that traffic decreased."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Business owners predicted chaos. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b35639a9b6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-128","passage":"<p>Some animals appear to understand basic numbers. In one experiment, researchers trained chicks that were only a few days old to find food behind a screen marked with a certain number of dots. When the number of dots stayed the same but the dots' size and spacing changed, the chicks still chose correctly most of the time. This suggests that the chicks were responding to quantity rather than to the overall appearance of the pattern.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how the chicks were fed.","It describes the age of the chicks.","It describes a test that helps distinguish responding to number from responding to appearance.","It contradicts the claim in the first sentence."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Changing size and spacing isolates quantity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.724e2ee922","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-131","passage":"<p>Many species of songbird learn their songs by listening to adults during a sensitive period early in life. Researchers raising young birds in soundproof rooms found that birds that never heard adult songs developed simple, unusual songs. Birds that heard recordings of their own species during the sensitive period developed normal songs, while those exposed only after the period ended did not.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents results showing that the timing of exposure matters.","It describes the birds' diet.","It explains how soundproof rooms work.","It contradicts the first sentence."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Exposure during versus after the sensitive period. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.8a5c0ac315","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-134","passage":"<p>Researchers studying ants noticed that when a colony's usual path to food was blocked, the ants quickly found a new route. Individual ants wandered somewhat randomly, but those that found food laid down chemical trails on the way back. Shorter routes were traveled more often, so their trails grew stronger, attracting more ants. In time, most ants followed the shortest available path, even though no single ant planned it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes an observation and then explains the process that produces it.","It compares ants with bees.","It describes how to remove ants from a home.","It argues that ants are smarter than other insects."],"answer":"A","explanation":"New routes found, then explained by trail reinforcement. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0205bc892f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-137","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My mother's garden in December / is only sticks and string, / the tags still marking rows / of what the ground will someday bring. / She walks it anyway each morning, / hands deep in her coat, / reading the empty beds / like a letter no one wrote.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final two lines of the poem?","choices":["They reveal that the mother has received a letter.","They describe the plants blooming in spring.","They explain why the garden failed.","They suggest that the mother finds meaning in the garden even when nothing is growing."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Reading empty beds like a letter implies imagined meaning. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7d0e30f1a5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-138","passage":"<p>Scientists investigating why some people get more mosquito bites than others found that mosquitoes are attracted partly by carbon dioxide and certain skin chemicals. People who produced more of particular fatty acids on their skin attracted mosquitoes far more strongly in lab tests, and this preference remained consistent when the same people were tested months apart.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"when the same people were tested months apart\" in the text?","choices":["It indicates that the difference in attractiveness was stable over time.","It describes how participants were chosen.","It explains how mosquitoes detect carbon dioxide.","It suggests that the tests were unreliable."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Consistency over months shows a lasting trait. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f516cd6770","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-140","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient village, they found that one house contained far more imported pottery, jewelry, and fine tools than the others. Some suggested that a wealthy chief lived there. Others noted that the house was also unusually large and contained many storage pits, and proposed that it may have been a communal building where valuable goods were kept for the whole village.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the village's construction methods.","It compares two villages.","It explains how pottery was imported.","It presents a finding and then describes two competing interpretations of it."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Unusual house, chief interpretation, communal interpretation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4ffc00c7fa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-141","passage":"<p>Some city governments have begun painting large murals on the walls of buildings in neighborhoods with high rates of graffiti. In several cities, murals were rarely tagged with graffiti, while plain walls nearby continued to be marked. Observers suggest that graffiti writers may respect the murals as art, or that the busy designs leave little blank space that invites tagging.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why graffiti is illegal.","It describes how murals are painted.","It contradicts the observation about murals.","It offers possible explanations for the pattern described earlier."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Respect for art or less blank space. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0e57a8d759","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-142","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My father repaired watches in a narrow shop with a single window. As a child, I thought his work was boring: hours bent over tiny gears, not speaking. Only when I tried it myself, years later, did I understand. The silence was not emptiness. It was concentration so complete that the rest of the world simply stepped aside.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents the narrator's childhood view, which she later revises.","It explains why the shop had one window.","It describes the tools her father used.","It reveals the narrator's current career."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"I thought his work was boring\" is revised later. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7abb169d80","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-143","passage":"<p>Many people assume that pandas eat bamboo because it is highly nutritious. In fact, bamboo is low in nutrients, and pandas digest only a small portion of what they eat. To survive, they eat for up to fourteen hours a day and conserve energy by moving relatively little. Their low-energy lifestyle is an adaptation to a diet that provides little fuel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It introduces a mistaken assumption that the rest of the text corrects.","It describes how pandas digest bamboo.","It summarizes the text's conclusion.","It explains why pandas live in forests."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"In fact, bamboo is low in nutrients.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4d99c52d52","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-145","passage":"<p>When a coastal town was hit by a severe storm, residents who lived near a restored salt marsh reported less flooding than those living near a stretch of shoreline protected only by a concrete wall. Scientists noted that the marsh's plants slowed incoming waves and absorbed water, and that, unlike the wall, the marsh had not been damaged by the storm.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It suggests that the marsh made flooding worse.","It describes how the concrete wall was built.","It presents residents' opinions about the storm.","It explains why the marsh provided better protection."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Plants slowed waves and absorbed water. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f54602cfd5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data6-149","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars believed that a certain medieval poem was the work of a single author. A computer analysis of word choices and sentence patterns found distinct differences between the first half of the poem and the second. Some scholars now suggest that a second poet completed the work, though others argue that a single author's style may simply have changed over many years of writing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It concludes that the analysis was flawed.","It presents two possible interpretations of the analysis's results.","It describes the poem's subject.","It explains how the computer analysis was performed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"A second poet or a changing style. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.74bdabf1ac","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-151","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every photograph in the hallway showed Grandmother smiling, but Lena had never once seen her smile in person. At the funeral, an old woman Lena did not recognize took her hand and said, \"Your grandmother laughed louder than anyone I ever knew, before the war.\" Lena looked back at the hallway photographs and noticed, for the first time, that all of them had been taken long ago.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It shows Lena connecting the stranger's comment to the photographs.","It explains why the grandmother stopped smiling.","It describes the funeral in detail.","It reveals that the photographs are of someone else."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Noticing their age links them to \"before the war.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.dbc8dd6521","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-159","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The lighthouse keeper had not spoken to another person in three weeks when the supply boat arrived. He had rehearsed what he would say: news about the storms, a request for more lamp oil. But when the young sailor jumped onto the dock and said hello, the keeper found that all he wanted was to hear the boy talk, about anything at all.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the keeper's practical plans, which contrast with what he actually wants.","It explains why the keeper lives alone.","It describes the supply boat.","It reveals the keeper's age."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Rehearsed topics contrast with his desire for simple talk. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.e5aabd9bce","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-160","passage":"<p>A tech company tested two versions of a sign-up page. One asked visitors for their name, email, phone number, and address. The other asked only for an email address. The shorter form had nearly twice as many sign-ups. The company concluded that each additional request for information discourages some visitors from completing the process.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a test comparing two options, reports the result, and states a conclusion.","It explains how to design websites.","It argues that email is outdated.","It compares two companies."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Two versions, result, and conclusion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.18805d49f6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-165","passage":"<p>Engineers designing bridges in earthquake zones once focused on making structures as rigid as possible. Newer designs often include flexible bearings and dampers that allow parts of a bridge to move slightly during shaking. By absorbing energy rather than resisting it, these features can reduce damage to the main structure.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It compares bridges with tunnels.","It contrasts an older design approach with a newer one and explains the newer approach's advantage.","It lists materials used in bridges.","It describes a famous bridge collapse."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Rigid versus flexible designs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.de1ae74f3d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-169","passage":"<p>A city experimented with \"complete streets,\" redesigning several roads to include wider sidewalks, bike lanes, and crosswalks with shorter crossing distances. Some drivers predicted longer commutes. Travel times for cars increased only slightly, while the number of pedestrians and cyclists on those streets rose substantially, and serious crashes declined.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how bike lanes were painted.","It presents a prediction that proved only partly accurate.","It summarizes the results of the redesign.","It explains why the city chose those streets."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Commutes lengthened \"only slightly.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.47aec4b511","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-170","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The old bridge knows the river's moods: / the spring flood's rushing rage, / the summer's lazy silver hum, / the winter's frozen stage. / It does not fight the water, / it simply holds its ground, / and lets each season pass beneath / without a single sound.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the poem?","choices":["It describes a journey across the bridge.","It narrates the construction of the bridge.","It lists the river's seasonal changes and then describes the bridge's steady response.","It compares a river with an ocean."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Seasons first, then the bridge's endurance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2e7f02412c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-172","passage":"<p>Many desert plants have waxy coatings on their leaves. The wax reduces water loss by sealing the leaf surface. Some plants in rain forests also have waxy leaves, but for a different reason: the slick surface helps rainwater run off quickly, preventing mold and algae from growing on the leaves.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a trait in one environment and then explains how the same trait serves a different purpose elsewhere.","It explains how wax is produced.","It argues that rain forest plants should grow in deserts.","It compares deserts and oceans."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Desert purpose, then rain forest purpose. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.6b00cd378c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data7-174","passage":"<p>Scientists once thought that adult brains could not produce new neurons. Studies of songbirds, and later of mammals, found evidence that new neurons form in certain brain regions throughout life. In some animals, the formation of new neurons increased with learning and exercise, though researchers continue to debate how much this occurs in adult humans.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final clause, beginning with \"though,\" in the text?","choices":["It notes an unresolved question about the findings.","It proves that humans form many new neurons.","It explains why scientists held the earlier belief.","It describes how songbirds learn."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Human extent remains debated. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b4ab0c4f33","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-178","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The old clock in the hallway had stopped at 4:17 the day Grandfather died, and no one had wound it since. Years passed. Then, on the morning of her own wedding, Tessa found her father standing in front of it with the key in his hand. He wound it slowly, and as the pendulum began to swing, he said, without turning around, \"It's time.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It establishes the clock's connection to a loss, giving meaning to the father's later action.","It explains how clocks are wound.","It reveals Tessa's age.","It describes the wedding ceremony."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The clock stopped when Grandfather died. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.8282bafba1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-179","passage":"<p>Researchers studying how people navigate cities found that residents who relied heavily on GPS directions formed less accurate mental maps of their neighborhoods than residents who navigated without it. When asked to draw maps or estimate distances between landmarks, GPS users made more errors, though they reached destinations just as reliably.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final clause, beginning with \"though,\" in the text?","choices":["It suggests that the study's findings were incorrect.","It explains how the researchers drew maps.","It notes that GPS users' ability to reach destinations was not impaired.","It describes which GPS devices were used."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Their spatial knowledge differed, not their arrival. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5f61a8099b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-180","passage":"<p>For centuries, scholars assumed that a certain carved stone monument was a religious object. Recent analysis of the carvings revealed markings that align with the positions of the sun at the solstices and equinoxes. Some researchers now propose that the monument may also have served as a calendar, helping communities plan planting and festivals.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It restates the traditional interpretation.","It describes how the monument was carved.","It explains why festivals were held.","It presents new evidence that leads to a revised interpretation."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Solar markings prompt the calendar idea. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4964f51552","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-181","passage":"<p>When a hospital began having pharmacists review patients' medication lists at discharge, errors in prescriptions sent home with patients dropped sharply. Pharmacists often caught medications that duplicated one another or could interact harmfully, problems that busy doctors had missed. Readmissions related to medication problems also declined.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the hospital's history.","It explains why patients were readmitted before the hospital opened.","It explains how pharmacists reduced errors.","It presents a drawback of the program."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They caught duplications and interactions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5fc2e38f37","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-182","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is adapted from an essay.</p><p>As a child I believed that adults knew exactly what they were doing. My parents paid bills, fixed leaks, and made decisions with what looked like total confidence. It was only when I became an adult myself that I realized how much of that confidence was performance, a steady face kept up so that children would feel safe while the grown-ups figured things out.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It lists household chores in order.","It describes a childhood belief and then the adult realization that revised it.","It argues that children should pay bills.","It compares two families."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Belief, then realization. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d5449c0df2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-183","passage":"<p>Ecologists found that frogs in ponds near busy roads call at higher pitches than frogs of the same species in quiet ponds. Traffic noise is concentrated at low frequencies, and higher-pitched calls may be easier for other frogs to hear over that noise. However, females of the species generally prefer lower-pitched calls, which typically come from larger males.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how frogs lay eggs.","It supports the idea that higher calls attract more mates.","It explains why roads are noisy.","It introduces a possible drawback of the change in calling behavior."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Females prefer lower calls. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.104fb03391","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-184","passage":"<p>A company that produces breakfast cereal tested whether placing a cartoon character on the box affected children's choices. In a taste test, children were given the same cereal in two boxes, one with a character and one without. Most children said the cereal from the character box tasted better, even though the cereal was identical.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"even though the cereal was identical\" in the text?","choices":["It suggests the test was unfair.","It emphasizes that the difference in ratings came from the packaging, not the food.","It explains how the cereal was made.","It describes the children's ages."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Identical food means packaging caused the difference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.fb9653d75a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-185","passage":"<p>When scientists first proposed that the continents slowly move, many geologists objected because no one could explain what force could push such enormous masses. Decades later, maps of the ocean floor revealed long underwater mountain ranges where new crust forms and spreads outward. This discovery provided a mechanism, and the idea of moving continents gained broad acceptance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that the continents never moved.","It compares mountains on land and underwater.","It describes how maps are made.","It describes an idea, the main objection to it, and the evidence that eventually addressed the objection."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Proposal, objection, and mechanism discovered. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.10ced683bd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-187","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Kenji's father spoke little English and rarely came to school events. So when Kenji saw him standing at the back of the auditorium during the science fair, still in his work uniform, Kenji nearly dropped his poster. His father did not ask questions or read the display. He simply stood beside it for the entire evening, nodding at every visitor who stopped.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reveals the winner of the science fair.","It describes Kenji's science project.","It explains why the father's attendance is surprising.","It explains why the father wears a uniform."],"answer":"C","explanation":"He rarely came to school events. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ad6798b34b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-189","passage":"<p>A museum that offered free admission one evening a month found that the free nights attracted visitors who had never been to the museum before, including many families with young children. Membership sign-ups also rose after these evenings, suggesting that some first-time visitors returned as paying members.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes a further effect that suggests free evenings had lasting benefits.","It explains why families have young children.","It describes the museum's exhibits.","It contradicts the first sentence."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Membership sign-ups rose. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.e6d2f7c100","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-192","passage":"<p>Researchers analyzing thousands of job postings found that ads using words such as \"competitive\" and \"dominant\" attracted fewer applications from women than ads describing the same jobs with words such as \"collaborative\" and \"supportive.\" When a company revised its ads to use more neutral language, the share of women applying increased.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the salary for the jobs.","It describes an intervention that supports the pattern found in the analysis.","It contradicts the first finding.","It explains why companies post jobs online."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Revised ads increased women's applications. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.e28f55292c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data8-195","passage":"<p>Some islands have species of birds that cannot fly, even though their ancestors could. Biologists suggest that on islands without ground predators, flying offered few advantages while requiring a great deal of energy. Over many generations, birds that spent less energy on flight muscles and more on other traits may have survived and reproduced more successfully.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It contradicts the first sentence.","It offers an explanation for the loss of flight described in the first sentence.","It explains how islands form.","It describes the diet of island birds."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Few advantages and high energy costs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.283f227597","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-198","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The letter from her brother arrived on the first day of spring, three years after their last argument. Mira read the single line twice—\"I planted the tomatoes the way you always said\"—and then sat down on the porch step and laughed until she cried. Their fight had been about something far larger than tomatoes, but she understood exactly what he meant.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reveals that the argument was about gardening.","It describes Mira's porch.","It clarifies that the letter's meaning goes beyond its literal words.","It explains how to plant tomatoes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The fight was larger than tomatoes, yet she understands the message. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3e9c425730","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-201","passage":"<p>A psychologist studying how people judge the passage of time asked participants to estimate how long they had spent on various tasks. People consistently felt that time passed faster when they were absorbed in engaging activities and slower when they were bored or waiting, even when the actual durations were identical.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"even when the actual durations were identical\" in the text?","choices":["It suggests that the study was flawed.","It explains how participants chose tasks.","It emphasizes that the difference lay in perception rather than in actual time.","It shows that the tasks lasted different amounts of time."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Identical durations isolate perception. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.945e48bf83","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-203","passage":"<p>A city that offered residents free compost bins found that participation varied widely by neighborhood. In areas where volunteers went door to door explaining how composting works, many more households used the bins than in areas where bins were simply delivered with printed instructions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It identifies a factor associated with the differences in participation.","It describes the size of the compost bins.","It explains why the city offered free bins.","It argues that composting is difficult."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Volunteer visits versus printed instructions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2fd43ae5c5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-204","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My first apartment had a window that looked directly at a brick wall. For a year I resented it. Then one spring a pair of sparrows built a nest in a gap between the bricks, and I found myself waking early to watch them carry grass and string. By the time the chicks fledged, I had stopped thinking of it as a bad view.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that brick walls are beautiful.","It describes an initial frustration, an event that changed it, and the narrator's new attitude.","It compares two apartments.","It describes how sparrows build nests."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Resentment, sparrows, and changed perspective. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0c167467f0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-205","passage":"<p>Astronomers studying a distant star noticed that its brightness dipped irregularly and by unusually large amounts, unlike the small, regular dips caused by orbiting planets. Some proposed that swarms of comets or clouds of dust were passing in front of it. Later observations showing that the dimming was stronger in blue light than red light supported the dust explanation, since fine dust blocks blue light more.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that planets cause all dimming.","It describes how telescopes measure color.","It describes an unusual observation, possible explanations, and evidence favoring one of them.","It compares two stars."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Observation, proposals, and supporting evidence. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.cbeb2b0918","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-208","passage":"<p>When a school replaced traditional detention with a program in which students discussed what led to their behavior and how to make amends, repeat offenses declined. Teachers noted that students were more likely to apologize and less likely to see discipline as unfair. Some teachers, however, felt the conversations took too much time during busy school days.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how detention used to work.","It presents a concern about the program.","It describes the program's benefits.","It argues that the program should expand."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"However,\" some teachers found it time-consuming. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.75832aaf2c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-209","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Every summer, Grandpa Luis entered the county fair's pie contest, and every summer he lost to Mrs. Delgado. This year, when the judges announced that he had finally won, he did not cheer. He walked straight to Mrs. Delgado's table, cut a slice of her peach pie, and said, loudly enough for everyone to hear, \"Still the best in the county.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It establishes a long rivalry that gives meaning to Grandpa Luis's reaction.","It explains how pies are judged.","It reveals the contest's prize.","It describes Mrs. Delgado's recipe."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Years of losing set up the gracious response. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.697c32e327","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data9-212","passage":"<p>Historians studying diaries from an eighteenth-century city found that residents frequently mentioned the price of bread. Entries describing price increases often appeared shortly before records of public protests. Some historians argue that the cost of basic food played a larger role in the city's unrest than political speeches, which receive more attention in traditional accounts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the city's government.","It summarizes political speeches.","It explains how diaries were preserved.","It presents an argument based on the diary evidence that challenges traditional emphasis."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Food costs versus political speeches. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3aa9cf5764","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-216","passage":"<p>Many hikers assume that a trail's difficulty depends mainly on its length. A park service that surveyed visitors found that elevation gain and uneven footing predicted how tiring people found a trail far better than distance did. Several short, steep, rocky trails were rated harder than much longer trails over gentle ground. The park revised its trail signs to list elevation gain alongside distance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It summarizes the park's response.","It describes the park's most popular trail.","It presents a common assumption that the survey results challenge.","It explains how trail signs are made."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Hikers assume length matters most. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.e4dd2cf9f7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-217","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>For years, Ines had walked past the abandoned greenhouse on her way to work, noting its broken panes and the vines pressing against the glass. One morning she stopped, pushed open the rusted door, and stepped inside. Among the weeds, a single orange tree still stood, heavy with small, bright fruit, as if it had been waiting all this time for someone to notice.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It reveals who owns the greenhouse.","It establishes the greenhouse's neglect, which makes the discovery inside surprising.","It explains why Ines changed jobs.","It describes the orange tree in detail."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Broken panes and vines suggest abandonment. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.33ea88f136","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-219","passage":"<p>When a city first installed bike lanes painted on the road, many cyclists still felt unsafe riding next to fast traffic. The city later tested lanes separated from cars by concrete barriers. On streets with protected lanes, the number of cyclists rose sharply, especially among people who described themselves as cautious riders.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes a limitation of one approach and the improved results of another.","It explains how concrete barriers are made.","It compares cycling in two cities.","It argues that bicycles should be banned."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Painted lanes fell short; protected lanes succeeded. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f19d0c4fa8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-221","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandfather taught me to fish on a lake so quiet that you could hear a dragonfly land. He never seemed to care whether we caught anything. When I asked him why we kept coming back on days when the fish would not bite, he pointed his rod at the mist rising off the water and said, \"We're not really here for the fish.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It establishes the peaceful setting that the grandfather values.","It explains why the fish did not bite.","It describes how to catch fish.","It introduces a conflict between the narrator and grandfather."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The quiet lake sets up his point. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.63d5a9c955","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-222","passage":"<p>When an online retailer began showing delivery dates rather than shipping speeds, such as \"arrives Thursday\" instead of \"ships in three to five days,\" customers were more likely to complete purchases. Researchers suggest that a specific date is easier for shoppers to understand and plan around than a range of days.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It offers an explanation for the change in customer behavior.","It contradicts the first sentence.","It explains how prices were set.","It describes how packages are shipped."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Dates are easier to plan around. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f10b1b35e3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-227","passage":"<p>A study of restaurant tipping found that servers who wrote \"thank you\" on customers' bills received slightly higher tips on average than servers who did not. The effect was small but consistent across many meals. The researchers suggest that small gestures of friendliness can influence how people respond, even in routine transactions.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"The effect was small but consistent\" in the text?","choices":["It qualifies the finding while indicating that it was reliable.","It describes how bills were printed.","It explains why customers tip.","It shows that the study failed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Small size, reliable pattern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.18147ae594","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-229","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>The night before the move, Sara walked through the empty house one last time. Without furniture, every room sounded different, her footsteps echoing where the couch and bookshelves had once absorbed them. In her old bedroom, she pressed her hand against the wall where she had marked her height each birthday, the pencil lines still faintly visible, then turned off the light.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It emphasizes how different the house feels now that it is empty.","It describes the new house Sara will move to.","It explains why the family is moving.","It lists the furniture that was sold."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Echoing footsteps show the change. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.3ff5320dc5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data10-230","passage":"<p>Botanists studying seeds stored in a frozen seed bank found that most seeds remained capable of sprouting after decades, but the rate varied widely by species. Some seeds lost viability quickly even when frozen, suggesting that seed banks may need different storage methods, or more frequent replanting and collection, for certain plants.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes an exception and its implication for seed bank practices.","It restates that most seeds survive.","It explains how seed banks are built.","It describes the history of seed banks."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some seeds lose viability quickly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.29192d45cb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-231","passage":"<p>When a regional railway added quiet cars, where phone calls and loud conversations were not allowed, officials expected them to appeal mainly to business travelers. Surveys found that the cars were popular with a wide range of passengers, including students studying for exams and parents with sleeping infants. The railway later added a second quiet car to its busiest routes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why trains are noisy.","It summarizes the railway's decision.","It presents an expectation that the survey results broadened.","It describes the railway's ticket prices."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Officials expected business travelers only. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d3ed6d67d9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-232","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The piano had been out of tune for years, and no one in the house played it anymore. When Aiko's grandmother moved in, she said nothing about it for a week. Then one evening, after everyone had gone to bed, the family heard music drifting up the stairs: an old, slightly sour-sounding waltz, played slowly, as if the player were remembering it one note at a time.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes Aiko's musical training.","It reveals that the grandmother dislikes music.","It explains how pianos are tuned.","It establishes the piano's neglect, which makes the later music meaningful."],"answer":"D","explanation":"No one had played it in years. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.31bb3b4379","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-233","passage":"<p>Researchers studying why some people are \"morning people\" and others \"night owls\" found that genetic differences affect the body's internal clock. However, when volunteers spent a week camping without artificial light, their sleep schedules shifted earlier and became more similar to one another, suggesting that modern lighting widens the natural differences between people.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It contradicts the idea that genes matter.","It describes how volunteers set up tents.","It presents a finding showing that environment also affects sleep timing.","It restates the genetic finding."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Without artificial light, schedules converged. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d25a3ea114","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-234","passage":"<p>When a town replaced a busy four-way stop with a small roundabout, some residents worried that drivers would be confused. For the first few weeks, traffic moved hesitantly. Within months, however, waiting times at the intersection dropped, and the number of collisions fell, as drivers adjusted to yielding while moving slowly through the circle.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It explains the history of roundabouts.","It describes a change, an early concern and difficulty, and the eventual benefits.","It argues that four-way stops are safer.","It compares two towns' roads."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Concern and hesitation, then improvement. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.807742fb4c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-237","passage":"<p>A study of online classes found that students who were required to post short weekly reflections about what they had learned were more likely to finish the course than students in sections without the requirement. Researchers suggest that the reflections kept students engaged and helped instructors notice early when students were struggling.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how the course was graded.","It contradicts the finding.","It offers possible explanations for the finding.","It explains why online classes exist."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Engagement and early detection. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.f2f0424741","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-239","passage":"<p>When a city began tracking which streetlights were broken using reports from residents' smartphones, repair times dropped from weeks to days. Previously, broken lights were often discovered only during periodic inspections. The reports also revealed that some neighborhoods had many more broken lights than others, prompting the city to investigate the cause.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the cost of streetlights.","It explains how smartphones work.","It shows that repair times increased.","It describes an additional insight gained from the reporting system."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Uneven distribution prompted investigation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ebef677b7b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-241","passage":"<p>When a high school changed its start time from 7:30 to 8:30 a.m., students reported sleeping about forty minutes more on school nights. Attendance in first-period classes improved, and the number of students falling asleep in class decreased. Some parents, however, noted that later dismissal times made it harder to schedule after-school activities and jobs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes students' sleep habits in detail.","It presents a drawback of the change.","It explains why the change was made.","It summarizes the attendance data."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Later dismissal complicated activities. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7bb8af3d56","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-242","passage":"<p>Some art restorers once used strong chemicals to remove old varnish from paintings, occasionally damaging the original paint beneath. Newer methods use gels that release cleaning agents slowly and can be applied to tiny areas under a microscope. These techniques allow restorers to remove grime while preserving delicate brushwork.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It describes the history of a single painting.","It explains how varnish is made.","It contrasts an older, risky method with a newer, more precise one.","It argues that paintings should never be restored."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Old harsh chemicals, new controlled gels. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b51f083101","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-246","passage":"<p>When a grocery chain began placing water bottles next to soda in refrigerated cases at checkout, sales of bottled water increased while soda sales dipped slightly. Previously, water had been stocked only in a distant aisle. The chain's analysts concluded that convenient placement influenced which drink shoppers grabbed at the last moment.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how soda is made.","It presents the analysts' conclusion.","It describes the earlier placement for comparison.","It describes prices at the store."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Water was previously in a distant aisle. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.462cf22160","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-247","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My father's workshop still smells of pine, / though he has not lifted a plane in years. / His tools hang in their outlines on the wall, / each shape drawn in pencil, / so that anything taken / would leave a ghost behind / until it came back home.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final three lines of the poem?","choices":["They list the tools on the wall.","They describe the father's new hobby.","They describe a ghost that lives in the workshop.","They explain the purpose of the outlines while suggesting absence and return."],"answer":"D","explanation":"A missing tool \"would leave a ghost.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ddc65afd55","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data11-252","passage":"<p>Some cities have painted crosswalks with bright, artistic designs. In one city, researchers compared intersections before and after the paintings were added and found that drivers yielded to pedestrians more often at painted crosswalks. The researchers suggest that the eye-catching designs made crosswalks more noticeable.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It argues against painting crosswalks.","It describes how the crosswalks were painted.","It offers an explanation for the change in driver behavior.","It lists the cities involved."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The designs were more noticeable. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.bbda2405cf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-254","passage":"<p>When a city began lending air conditioners to elderly residents during heat waves, emergency room visits for heat-related illness among older adults dropped noticeably. Social workers delivering the units also checked on residents' health and connected several with other services, such as meal delivery, that they had not known about.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how air conditioners cool rooms.","It describes the city's weather.","It presents a drawback of the program.","It describes an additional benefit that came from the program's deliveries."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Health checks and service referrals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.feeb715d9a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-255","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>Every morning, Omar's father left a folded note in his lunch bag, usually a joke so bad that Omar groaned aloud in the cafeteria. By high school, Omar pretended to be embarrassed and threw the notes away before his friends could see. Years later, packing to leave for college, he found a shoebox in his father's closet filled with copies of every note, each one dated in careful handwriting.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the jokes were bad.","It describes Omar's friends.","It reveals where Omar went to college.","It shows Omar's apparent dismissal of the notes, which contrasts with the later discovery."],"answer":"D","explanation":"He threw notes away, while his father kept copies. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.4c95253955","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-256","passage":"<p>Researchers studying wild chimpanzees observed that groups in different regions use different tools and techniques, such as cracking nuts with stones in some areas but not in others where the same nuts and stones are available. Because these differences are not explained by the environment alone, some researchers describe them as cultural traditions passed down through learning.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"where the same nuts and stones are available\" in the text?","choices":["It explains how stones crack nuts.","It describes the chimpanzees' diet.","It rules out a lack of resources as the explanation for the differences.","It suggests that chimpanzees cannot find stones."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Same resources, different behavior. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.8172f36591","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-259","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a memoir.</p><p>My grandmother spoke four languages but never learned to drive. So every Saturday, I drove her to the market across town, and every Saturday she used a different language: Portuguese with the fish seller, French with the baker, Italian with the woman who sold olives. In the car on the way home, she taught me one new word from each conversation.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It introduces details about the grandmother that set up the weekly routine.","It reveals the narrator's age.","It explains why the market was far away.","It lists the foods sold at the market."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Four languages and no driving lead to Saturday trips. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0c7e1fa9cc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-261","passage":"<p>When archaeologists excavated an ancient harbor, they found remains of ships loaded with pottery made in regions hundreds of kilometers away. Some pottery contained traces of olive oil and wine. The discoveries suggest that the harbor was part of a trade network that connected distant communities long before historians had assumed such networks existed in the region.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how ships were built.","It provides details about goods that were likely traded.","It contradicts the first sentence.","It explains why the harbor was abandoned."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Olive oil and wine residues. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.808b48e5ef","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-262","passage":"<p>When a town created a community tool library where residents could borrow ladders, drills, and garden equipment, organizers expected mainly homeowners to use it. Many borrowers turned out to be renters who lacked storage space and did not want to buy tools they would use only occasionally. The library also became a place where neighbors shared repair tips.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It contradicts the earlier sentences.","It describes the tools in detail.","It explains why renters lack storage space.","It describes an additional role the library came to play."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sharing repair tips. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.9da4d3df23","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-263","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>The kite pulls at my hand / as if it knows the sky / belongs to it, and I / am only holding the string. / I could let go. / It would climb and climb, / and I would lose it, / and I think, some days, / that would be enough.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the line \"I could let go\" in the poem?","choices":["It reveals that the string broke.","It marks a turn from describing the kite's pull to considering releasing it.","It describes the wind's direction.","It explains how kites are made."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The poem shifts to contemplating release. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.fb7df967c9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-265","passage":"<p>Scientists studying a species of songbird found that birds living on islands with fewer predators had duller feathers than birds of the same species on the mainland. Bright colors can attract mates but also make birds easier for predators to spot. The researchers suggest that the balance between attracting mates and avoiding predators differs between the two environments.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains the competing effects of bright coloration.","It explains how feathers grow.","It contradicts the first sentence.","It describes the islands' vegetation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Attracts mates but increases visibility. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2792a9eb87","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-266","passage":"<p>When a public library began offering free museum passes that patrons could reserve, the passes were booked months in advance. Surveys found that many families who used them had never visited those museums because of ticket costs. Several museums reported that some pass users later returned as paying visitors or members.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains why the passes were so popular with some families.","It presents a drawback of the program.","It explains the museums' opening hours.","It describes how passes were printed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Cost had kept families away. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.7c05f14706","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-267","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>On the last day of camp, Theo's counselor handed him the old compass they had used on every hike. Theo protested that it belonged to the camp. The counselor shook his head. \"It belongs to whoever pays attention to where they're going,\" he said. Theo turned the compass over in his hands, noticing for the first time a list of initials scratched into the back.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It shows that Theo is disappointed.","It reveals that the compass has been passed to others before Theo.","It explains how compasses work.","It describes the camp's hiking trails."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Initials indicate previous owners. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.b4506e81a3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data12-268","passage":"<p>When a regional water utility sent customers monthly reports comparing their water use with that of similar neighboring households, average water use fell by several percent. Households that had used more than their neighbors reduced their use the most. A few households using less than their neighbors, however, slightly increased their use after seeing the comparison.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes an unexpected response among some households.","It describes the utility's history.","It restates the main finding.","It explains how water is billed."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Low users increased use slightly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.d96fe23700","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-271","passage":"<p>When a small city replaced its paper parking tickets with a smartphone payment system, officials expected mainly convenience benefits. They also discovered that the data showed which blocks were almost always full and which were rarely used. The city then adjusted prices block by block, raising them where spaces were scarce and lowering them where spaces sat empty, and drivers found open spaces more easily.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes an unexpected benefit that made the later policy possible.","It presents a drawback of the app.","It describes the city's history.","It explains how smartphones process payments."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Usage data revealed demand patterns. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.242d943303","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-274","passage":"<p>When a university library began staying open twenty-four hours during exam weeks, administrators expected crowded nights. Usage between midnight and six a.m. was lower than expected, but those who came reported that the quiet space helped them focus. The library kept the extended hours but reduced staffing during the least busy periods.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how the library responded to the mixed results.","It explains why exams are stressful.","It contradicts the survey results.","It describes the library's architecture."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Kept hours, reduced staff. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.801cc2f18e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-275","passage":"<p>Scientists studying the sounds of healthy forests found that dawn choruses of birds were louder and more varied in forests with greater biodiversity. In logged or degraded forests, recordings contained fewer species and longer silences. The researchers suggest that automated audio recorders could help monitor forest health across large, remote areas.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes how recorders are made.","It explains why birds sing at dawn.","It proposes a practical application of the findings.","It contradicts the earlier findings."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Monitoring with recorders. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.c9d28c1f37","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-279","passage":"<p>When a community garden added raised beds at wheelchair height, organizers hoped to include gardeners with disabilities. The beds proved popular with many older gardeners as well, who found them easier on their backs and knees. The garden later replaced several ground-level plots with raised beds because of demand.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It shows that the beds benefited more people than originally intended.","It describes the garden's vegetables.","It explains how raised beds are built.","It presents a drawback of the beds."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Older gardeners also benefited. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.fc9031559e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-280","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>Each autumn my mother pressed a leaf / between the pages of whatever book she read, / so that years later, opening a novel, / I'd find a maple, flat and red, / marking not a chapter / but a season of her life, / a small dry letter / from a year I barely knew.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"not a chapter / but a season of her life\" in the poem?","choices":["It describes the colors of maple leaves.","It explains how books are organized.","It shifts the leaf's meaning from a practical bookmark to a personal memento.","It reveals that the books were never read."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The leaf records time in her life. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ac5a3921e8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-281","passage":"<p>Researchers measuring air quality inside schools found that classrooms with poor ventilation had high levels of carbon dioxide by midday. Students in those rooms scored lower on tests of attention given in the afternoon. When ventilation was improved, carbon dioxide levels fell and afternoon test scores rose.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It describes the classroom furniture.","It contradicts the earlier findings.","It explains how tests were written.","It describes an intervention that strengthens the link between air quality and attention."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Improved ventilation raised scores. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.6ba2d68943","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-282","passage":"<p>When a town's historic movie theater faced closure, residents formed a nonprofit and raised money through small donations to buy the building. They showed older films, hosted local music performances, and rented the space for community events. Within five years, the theater was financially stable and had become a gathering place for the town.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?","choices":["It argues that theaters are unnecessary.","It describes a threat, the community's response, and the outcome.","It explains how films are projected.","It compares two theaters."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Closure threat, nonprofit efforts, and stability. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.a4d99c1dc3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-284","passage":"<p>When a hospital began allowing family members to stay overnight with patients in the intensive care unit, some staff worried that visitors would interfere with care. Instead, nurses reported that families often noticed changes in patients' conditions and alerted staff early. Patients and families also reported lower stress levels.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a concern that the later results did not support.","It explains how intensive care units work.","It summarizes the results.","It describes nurses' training."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Staff worried about interference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ce58e115e1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-285","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>Leo had practiced his speech for the class election until he knew every word. At the podium, he looked out at his classmates, and the words vanished. After a long, awful pause, he said the only thing he could think of: \"I forgot my speech. But I remember what I wanted to say.\" Then he told them, plainly, why he wanted to help. The room was quiet when he finished, and then it applauded.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It establishes Leo's preparation, which makes his forgetting more dramatic.","It reveals the other candidates' names.","It explains why Leo ran for office.","It describes the election results."],"answer":"A","explanation":"He knew every word before forgetting. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.2364a2d7c3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-288","passage":"<p>Ornithologists studying crows in a city found that the birds dropped hard-shelled nuts onto crosswalks and waited for cars to crack them. The crows then retrieved the nut pieces when traffic lights stopped the cars. Researchers noted that the birds appeared to time their retrieval to the light signals.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It highlights a detail suggesting the crows' behavior is especially sophisticated.","It contradicts the first sentence.","It describes the city's traffic laws.","It explains how nuts grow."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Timing with light signals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.5c65153d63","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-290","passage":"<p>When a regional power grid began paying households with rooftop solar panels and home batteries to share stored electricity during peak demand, the grid needed fewer backup power plants on hot afternoons. Households earned money, and the grid avoided building new capacity. Some households, however, worried that their batteries would wear out faster.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It presents a concern some participants had about the program.","It explains how solar panels work.","It describes the grid's history.","It restates the program's benefits."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Battery wear worries. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.986b842fee","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-295","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a novel.</p><p>The fog rolled in every afternoon at four, as reliably as a train. Newcomers complained that it ruined their plans. The fishermen, though, set their clocks by it, pulling in their nets as the gray wall crept over the harbor. \"The fog isn't the problem,\" old Marisol liked to say. \"Expecting the sun is.\"</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It expresses a view that frustration comes from unrealistic expectations rather than the fog itself.","It describes the fishermen's nets.","It reveals that the fog will stop.","It explains how fog forms."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Expecting the sun is\" the problem. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.fa9f996d75","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-296","passage":"<p>When a school district replaced sugary drinks in vending machines with water and milk, researchers expected students might simply buy sodas elsewhere. Surveys found that overall soda consumption among students did decline, though some students did bring drinks from home. Consumption fell most among younger students, who had fewer opportunities to buy drinks off campus.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final sentence in the text as a whole?","choices":["It explains how milk is produced.","It identifies which students' consumption changed most and offers a reason.","It describes the vending machines.","It contradicts the survey results."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Younger students had fewer off-campus options. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.eb3d9089aa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-299","passage":"<p>When a city introduced a program allowing residents to report potholes through a phone app with photos and locations, repair crews could plan efficient routes and fix clusters of potholes in a single trip. Average repair times fell, and residents reported greater satisfaction with road maintenance.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the phrase \"with photos and locations\" in the text?","choices":["It explains how phones take pictures.","It describes the city's roads.","It identifies the information that allowed crews to plan efficient routes.","It suggests the app was difficult to use."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Precise data enabled planning. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.0bccae43a9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-300","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a poem.</p><p>My grandmother's hands / were never still: / shelling peas, / mending hems, / braiding my hair / tight enough to last a week. / Even in the hospital, / her fingers moved across the blanket, / looking for something to do.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the final three lines of the poem?","choices":["They describe a new hobby.","They contrast her with other patients.","They explain why she was in the hospital.","They show that the grandmother's lifelong habit continued even in illness."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fingers still searching for work. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.text-structure.2.ba9d452ba9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"text-structure","difficulty":2,"group":"psg-passage_data13-302","passage":"<p class=\"src\">The following text is from a short story.</p><p>For weeks, Nadia's little brother had begged to come along on her early-morning runs, and for weeks she had said no. He was too slow, she told him. One Saturday she finally gave in. He fell behind within a block, red-faced and panting, but he did not stop. At the end of the route, Nadia found herself jogging in place, waiting, and cheering louder than she ever had for herself.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the function of the sentence \"He was too slow, she told him\" in the text?","choices":["It describes the running route.","It reveals the brother's age.","It gives Nadia's reason for refusing, which contrasts with her later response.","It explains why the brother wanted to run."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Her dismissal contrasts with her cheering. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
